---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Every claim here is one the code and tests already hold: tests/e2e/privacy.spec.ts (no file or text leaves the page),
// src/ui/track.ts (the only analytics events and their props), src/ai/loader.ts (models kept in the browser's cache).
const repo = 'https://github.com/jami-yashwanth/jiffkit';
// The analytics section describes Umami only on builds that load it (the same switch as Base.astro).
const analytics = Boolean(import.meta.env.PUBLIC_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID);
---
<Base title="Privacy - jiffkit" description="What jiffkit does with your files and data: your files never leave your device, and analytics is cookieless." path="/privacy/">
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="subhead">Your files and text never leave your device. This page lists everything that does.</p>

  <h2>Your files and text</h2>
  <p>Every tool runs in your browser. Files you choose and text you paste are read, converted and saved on your device. They are never uploaded, and we never see them: not their contents, names or sizes.</p>
  <p>You can check this yourself: open your browser's developer tools, go to the Network tab and use any tool. The <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/tests/e2e/privacy.spec.ts`}>privacy test</a> in our source code does the same in the engines behind Chrome, Safari and Firefox on every change, and fails if a file name or a file's contents appear in any request.</p>

  <h2>What your browser downloads</h2>
  <p>The pages and their code come from jiffkit.com. The AI tools (Remove background, Upscale image, Transcribe audio, Image to text) also download a model the first time you use them. Those are plain downloads that send nothing about your files. Your browser keeps the models in its cache so the tools work offline afterwards. You can delete them by clearing this site's data in your browser.</p>

  <h2>Analytics</h2>
  {analytics ? (
    <>
      <p>We use <a href="https://umami.is">Umami</a> to count visits and see which tools are used. It sets no cookies, and it is turned off if your browser sends Do Not Track. Umami records the page you visited, the site you came from, your browser, device type and country. It does not store your IP address.</p>
      <p>The tools send three events, and these are the only details they carry:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A tool was used:</strong> the tool's name.</li>
        <li><strong>A conversion finished:</strong> the tool's name and how many files.</li>
        <li><strong>A conversion failed:</strong> the tool's name and a short reason, such as "too large" or "cancelled".</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Never a file name, size, type or contents, and never anything you typed or pasted.</p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <p>This copy of the site has no analytics.</p>
  )}

  <h2>Hosting</h2>
  <p>The site is hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Like any web host, Cloudflare handles your browser's requests for pages and may keep standard request logs, such as your IP address and browser, under <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">its own privacy policy</a>. We don't add to them.</p>

  <h2>No accounts, no ads</h2>
  <p>There is no sign-up, no login, no advertising and no selling of data. The tools are free.</p>

  <h2>Who runs jiffkit</h2>
  <p>jiffkit is built and run by Jami Yashwanth. The full source code is public under the MIT licence at <a href={repo}>github.com/jami-yashwanth/jiffkit</a>.</p>
  <p>Questions or concerns: <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p>

  <p class="updated">Last updated 29 October 2026.</p>
</Base>
<style>
  .updated {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }
</style>
